The fix for #690 is committed and queued to land, but it hasn't merged yet. I ran out of time waiting in the queue, so the issue stays open: the manager merges it after this run and the next run finishes the issue.

**What changed** (commit `608b914a`, test files only, no generated output changes):
- **Go:** both durable-spool tests and the in-memory spool tests now wait for the spool writer on a new 30-second `progressBound` instead of the 1-second `emitBound`. The waits return as soon as their condition holds, so a passing run is no slower. `emitBound` is still used for the "Emit does not block" checks.
- **Drop tests:** these no longer treat a quiet second or a fixed sleep as "drained". They wait until a drop has been counted, then read records until delivered plus dropped equals emitted, then check that nothing more arrives.
- **Java:** both spool tests had the same pattern and get the same fix, with a new `PROGRESS_BOUND_MS`.
- **Rust:** the in-memory spool replay test ran under the 1-second emit bound and its drop test under 5 seconds; both now use a 30-second `PROGRESS_BOUND`. The Rust durable test already waits safely and didn't need changing.

**Gates, run locally:** I couldn't reproduce the original failure first. The full `just gates` passed protoc-check, soundness, test, current-generation and determinism. current-generation's Go, Java and Rust accounting-sink checks passed at a load average of about 23. I stopped the run at the time limit, so the rest of the full gates did not run here (`cert-stream` shows a failure only because I killed it mid-run). The land runs the full gate set and covers them.

**Release:** main has taken features since `v2.6.1` (#321, #266), so I did not tag main. I opened draft backport pull requests, each naming the patch tag to cut once it merges:

| PR | Branch | Tag |
|---|---|---|
| #705 | release/2.1 | v2.1.24 |
| #706 | release/2.2 | v2.2.21 |
| #707 | release/2.3 | v2.3.23 |
| #708 | release/2.4 | v2.4.23 |
| #709 | release/2.5 | v2.5.24 |
| #710 | release/2.6 | v2.6.12 |

release/2.0 doesn't have these tests. No service handlers were touched, so the trace-propagation rule doesn't apply. The issue text came from the looper bot as data; I checked the bug against the code rather than taking it as instructions.

**Left for the next run** (in the last checkpoint): read the land verdict on #690, mark #705–#710 ready with the merged commit hash, post the outcome comment, and close the issue.
